<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Pulled into directional-derivatives.ptx by XInclude.
     Requires in assets/ :  isotherm-runner.html, isotherm-runner-preview.png -->

<subsection xml:id="subsec-scalar-fields-potentials">
  <title>Scalar Fields, Potentials, and the Direction Nature Chooses</title>

  <p>
    A <term>scalar field</term> is a rule that assigns a single number to every point of
    a region: the temperature <m>T(x,y)</m> in a city, the pressure <m>P(x,y)</m> over an
    ocean, the elevation <m>h(x,y)</m> on a hillside, the concentration <m>c(x,y)</m> of a
    pollutant in a corridor. A scalar field carries no direction of its own. It is only a
    number at each location.
  </p>
  
  <p>
  Physics, by contrast, is full of quantities that point somewhere: heat flows,
   forces push, wind blows. These are <term>vector fields</term>. The gradient is
   what ties them back to scalar fields <mdash/> at least for the
   <term>conservative</term> fields taken up in MATH 14 at SCU, where the whole
   vector field is recovered from a single scalar potential by differentiation.
   Because such fields are so common in nature, the pattern in the table below
   appears again and again across physics.
  </p>

  <p>
    When a scalar field governs a physical force or flux, we call the field a
    <term>potential</term>, and the associated vector quantity is, up to a constant, the
    <em>negative</em> gradient of that potential.
  </p>

  <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor">
    <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
      <cell>Setting</cell>
      <cell>Scalar field</cell>
      <cell>Vector quantity</cell>
      <cell>Level curves are</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>Heat conduction</cell>
      <cell>temperature <m>T</m></cell>
      <cell>heat flux <m>\mathbf q = -k\nabla T</m> (Fourier's law)</cell>
      <cell>isotherms</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>Electrostatics</cell>
      <cell>potential <m>V</m></cell>
      <cell>electric field <m>\mathbf E = -\nabla V</m></cell>
      <cell>equipotentials</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>Mechanics</cell>
      <cell>potential energy <m>U</m></cell>
      <cell>conservative force <m>\mathbf F = -\nabla U</m></cell>
      <cell>contours of constant energy</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>Meteorology</cell>
      <cell>pressure <m>P</m></cell>
      <cell>pressure-gradient force <m>-\nabla P</m> per unit volume</cell>
      <cell>isobars</cell>
    </row>
  </tabular>

  <p>
    The minus sign in each of these laws encodes one physical fact:
    <em>nature moves downhill</em>. Heat drains from hot to cold, charges move toward low
    electric potential, a ball rolls toward low gravitational potential energy, air accelerates 
    toward low pressure. So the direction the physics actually selects is the direction of most rapid
    <em>decrease</em>, which is exactly the case <m>\theta = \pi</m> of
    <m>D_{\mathbf u}f = \|\nabla f\|\cos\theta</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Three consequences carry over unchanged from
    <xref ref="sec-dirderiv-properties"/>, and they are worth restating in physical
    language.
  </p>

  <p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>\theta = 0</m>: fastest increase, at the rate <m>\|\nabla f\|</m>. This is the
        direction heat comes <em>from</em>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>\theta = \pi</m>: fastest decrease, at the rate <m>-\|\nabla f\|</m>. This is the
        direction the flux, the force, or the wind actually points.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>\theta = \pi/2</m>: no change at all. These directions trace the level curves of
        the field <mdash/> isotherms, equipotentials, isobars, the contour lines on a
        topographic map. Since <m>\nabla f</m> is orthogonal to the level curve through a
        point, <em>the flux, the force, or the wind always crosses the
        contours at right angles</em>.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
  </p>

  <example xml:id="ex-two-runners">
    <title>Two Runners in a City Heat Island</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        On a still summer afternoon the pavement downtown is far hotter than the outlying
        neighborhoods. Model the surface air temperature, in degrees Fahrenheit, by
        <md>T(x,y) = 74 + 14\,e^{-(x^2 + 3y^2)/40},</md>
        where <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are measured in miles east and north of downtown. The
        core reaches <m>88^\circ</m>F, and the outskirts settle near <m>74^\circ</m>F. Two
        runners meet at the point <m>P_0(1,1)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              The first runner is overheating and wants cooler air as quickly as possible.
              Which direction should she take, and how fast does the temperature fall along
              it?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The second runner has settled into the temperature at <m>P_0</m> and wants a
              route on which it never changes at all. What path should she run?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Differentiating the exponential with the chain rule,
        <md>
          <mrow>\nabla T \amp = -\frac{14}{40}\,e^{-(x^2+3y^2)/40}\left(2x\,\mathbf i + 6y\,\mathbf j\right),</mrow>
          <mrow>(\nabla T)_{(1,1)} \amp = -\frac{14\,e^{-1/10}}{40}\left(2\,\mathbf i + 6\,\mathbf j\right)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp \approx -0.633\,\mathbf i - 1.900\,\mathbf j .</mrow>
        </md>
        At <m>P_0</m> the temperature itself is
        <m>T(1,1) = 74 + 14e^{-1/10} \approx 86.7^\circ</m>F.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part 1.</term> Temperature falls fastest in the direction of
        <m>-\nabla T</m>, which is a positive multiple of <m>\mathbf i + 3\mathbf j</m>.
        Normalizing, the heading is north-northeast:
        <md>\mathbf u = \frac{1}{\sqrt{10}}\,\mathbf i + \frac{3}{\sqrt{10}}\,\mathbf j,</md>
        and the rate of change along it is
        <md>
          <mrow>D_{\mathbf u} T \amp = -\|\nabla T\|
                = -\frac{14\,e^{-1/10}}{40}\sqrt{4 + 36}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp \approx -2.0 \ \frac{{}^\circ\text{F}}{\text{mi}} .</mrow>
        </md>
        Notice how modest this is. The gradient hands her the <em>best available</em>
        direction, not a dramatic one: even running the optimal heading, she must cover a
        full mile to shed about two degrees. Any other heading does worse.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part 2.</term> The second runner needs <m>D_{\mathbf u}T = 0</m>, that is,
        <m>\mathbf u</m> orthogonal to <m>\nabla T</m>:
        <md>\mathbf u = \pm\frac{1}{\sqrt{10}}\left(3\,\mathbf i - \mathbf j\right).</md>
        Following that condition continuously, rather than for a single step, means never
        leaving the level curve of <m>T</m> through <m>P_0</m>. Since <m>T</m> is constant
        exactly when <m>x^2 + 3y^2</m> is constant, her route is
        <md>x^2 + 3y^2 = 4,</md>
        an ellipse with semi-axes <m>2</m> miles east-west and
        <m>2/\sqrt{3} \approx 1.15</m> miles north-south <mdash/> a closed loop of roughly
        ten miles, every step of it at <m>86.7^\circ</m>F.
      </p>

    <p>
      In the animation below, switch between the two runners. On the loop the thermometer
      holds at <m>86.7^\circ</m>F while the blue gradient arrow stays stubbornly
      perpendicular to her heading; on the escape route the same reading falls steadily, and
      the path cuts across every isotherm at a right angle.
    </p>
      <p>
        Use the interactive figure to explore the problem further geometrically,
        switching between the two routes and watching how the readings respond as the
        runner moves.
      </p>
    <figure xml:id="figure-isotherm-runner">
      <caption>
        Two routes from <m>P_0(1,1)</m> in the field
        <m>T(x,y) = 74 + 14e^{-(x^2+3y^2)/40}</m>. The highlighted ellipse is the
        level curve <m>x^2+3y^2=4</m>, on which <m>D_{\mathbf u}T = 0</m>.
      </caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-isotherm-runner"
                   iframe="isotherm-runner.html"
                   width="100%" aspect="6:5">
        <instructions>
          <p>
            Press <q>Run the isotherm</q> to send the runner around the level
            curve <m>x^2+3y^2=4</m>, or <q>Dash for cool air</q> to send her
            along the path of steepest cooling. The readout shows her
            temperature, position, directional derivative
            <m>D_{\mathbf u}T</m>, and distance travelled; the blue arrow is
            <m>\nabla T</m> at her position. Use <q>Pace</q> to change her
            speed, and <q>Pause</q> or <q>Reset</q> to control the animation.
          </p>
        </instructions>
      </interactive>
    </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>


</subsection>
